	ELECTRONIC FRONTIER CANADA (EFC) --- PRESS RELEASE
       
(For immediate release --- January 12, 1995)



   -- HUMOUR POLICE CRACK DOWN ON TASTELESSNESS AT U OF GUELPH --

    STUDENTS:  IT'S NO JOKE.  BE CAREFUL WHAT YOU SAY -- OR ELSE !

There's a definite chill in Guelph, and it's no laughing matter.
Students using computers at the University of Guelph are looking
over their shoulders, scared that Big Brother could expel them if
what they say in their electronic discussion groups is deemed "offensive".

A student faces possible expulsion from the university for running
a "tasteless jokes" conference on a university computer network
called T.COSY (Teaching Conference System).  There were almost 300 avid
readers when this conference was shut down just before Christmas and all
messages erased.

Someone apparently thought one of these jokes went too far.
Keith McIntyre, Director of Security Services, refuses to reveal the
nature of the offensive joke and refuses to say who complained.
"All will be revealed once the investigation is complete," says McIntyre.
He says it might be the end of January before the student, Carol Walberg,
learns whether she'll be asked to appear before the Judicial Review Committee.

The "tasteless jokes" in the conference ran the gamut, from "things to
say after you fart", to jokes making light of disabilities, feminists,
or racial stereotypes.  Walberg admits she didn't see the humour in some
of the Jewish jokes, "but I didn't feel it was up to me to censor them,"
she said.  A few people who didn't like the jokes `flamed' the author by
sending them nasty email."  In this sort of conference, unlike a newspaper
article or book, readers can easily reply to the author -- either privately
(by email) or publicly (in the same conference).

"What ever happened to the idea that a university was a safe place
to voice unpopular ideas or opinions?" wonders Walberg, who is
a 49 year-old sociology student.
"Now, students are afraid to speak freely because they know they're
under electronic surveillance."

"I think it's very inappropriate in a democratic society to prohibit
open discussions among willing participants, no matter what the
communication medium," says David Jones, who is a professor of computer
science at McMaster University and was speaking on behalf of
Electronic Frontier Canada (EFC), a group that wants to see privacy and
free speech safeguarded in cyberspace.

Jeff Shallit, co-founder of EFC and professor of computer science at
the University of Waterloo thinks Guelph has got it backwards,
"It's the University's duty to guarantee free expression,
not act as official censor.  Guelph's action is competely contrary
to the fundamental principles of a university."

"This has nothing to do with censorship and freedom of speech,"
says McIntyre, "it's a human rights issue.  It depends on the reader
[of the joke] and how they perceived it."

"It just doesn't make any sense," says Walberg.  "Most of the messages that
appeared in the T.COSY conference were just copied from the Usenet newsgroup
alt.tasteless.jokes!  That group, as well as alt.binaries.pictures.tasteless,
is still available to all Guelph students through the campus `news server'.
The University of Guelph news server carries 4,316 Usenet discussions groups
from around the world, on a very wide range of topics.[1]
Doug Blain, Director of Computing Services at Guelph, refuses to comment.

In a recent symposium sponsored by EFC, Mr. Justice John Sopinka of
Canada's Supreme Court hinted in his invited talk that actions like
those of the Guelph administration might be subject to a challenge
under the Charter of Rights and Freedoms.  Sopinka said that recent
Supreme Court decisions have "left open the possibility that some form
of activity by the University might constitute government action and thus,
be subject to the Charter.  If governing bodies engage in acts of
censorship, they run the risk of being classed as government action
and subject to the control of the Charter."

"Jokes can be serious stuff," says Jones with a straight face.
Several scholarly efforts to analyze the `theory of jokes' can be found
in the University of Guelph Library.  The book "Jokes: Form, Content,
Use, and Function" comes complete with diagrams, graphs, equations,
and of course, jokes -- some of them sexist or racist.
"Should that book be pulled of the shelves?" asks Jones rhetorically.

The student in the middle of this mess, Carol Walberg,
thinks this electronic censorship may
do some real harm.  "Sometimes cracking jokes is just one way people cope
with human tragedy.  If this form of expression is taken away, then people
may suffer because they're keeping their feelings bottled up inside."
 
