4) What is the story of the Homolka/Bernardo/Teale case?

(I must attribute, acknowledge and extend great thanks to both Brian
O'Neill and Michael Wass for providing the bulk of the information
contained in the next section.  Many of the facts below are excerpts from
O'Neill's upcoming book "Canada's Ken and Barbie Killers". - Ed)

a) The Scarborough Rapist

The location is Scarborough, Ontario.  This city of approximately 500,000
people forms the eastern chunk of the Municipality of Metropolitan Toronto,
Canada's largest city with an estimated population of 3,000,000.  In 1987,
a string of serious sexual assaults hit this city and brought an outcry
that resulted in increased safety awareness and a push by police to nab a
suspect.  A composite sketch, given by one victim, was presented to the
media.  Dubbed 'The Scarborough Rapist," the suspect in question was an
attractive young man with blonde hair.   Soon after the sketch was made
public, the assaults stopped.

b) Leslie Mahaffy

We move forward to early June, 1991. Several Burlington, Ontario teens were
out for a joyride on a hilly area road.  The car they were travelling in
lost control at high speed, and they were all killed. One person that knew
these dead teens was a pretty 14-year old girl named Leslie Erin Mahaffy.
Leslie lived with her parents Debbie and Dan and her (then) nine year-old
brother Ryan in Burlington. She was just finishing Grade 9 when the
accident occurred.  A wake was planned on June 15, 1991, at an area meeting
spot - a secluded park near a large rock.  Afterward, when Leslie returned
home, she had discovered that her mother had locked her out of the house,
so she went to a nearby variety store to use the payphone.  That was the
last time she was seen alive.

Several weeks later, on June 29, the day after what would have been her
fifteenth birthday, her cement-encased, dismembered body was found in Lake
Gibson (an nearby lake)  by some fishermen. The water levels had lowered
and her cement blocks became visible. On the same day her remains were
found, not 20 miles away, one Paul Kenneth Bernardo married Karla Homolka
in a church located in Niagara-on-the-Lake, Ontario.

c) Kristen French
 
Ten months later, on April 16, 1992, 15-year-old Grade 10 student Kristen
Dawn French was kidnapped from a church parking lot in St. Catharines,
Ontario, another area city.  The popular daughter of Doug and Donna French
was returning home from school when she went missing.  Her classmates took
to wearing green ribbons in the hope of her safe return.  However, on April
30, her nude body had been found in a ditch on a wooded side road outside
Burlington. French's long dark hair had been shorn off and she had been
sexually abused.  Police said she was kept alive for 13 of the 14 days
since her abduction.

One of the most intensive manhunts in Canadian history was launched. One of
the biggest clues came from a man named Constantine Ganga, who reported
that an elderly couple were usually in the area of the abduction in the
time frame reported.  Later, a Hamilton girl reported being watched, around
the date of the French abduction, by two men in a cream colored Camaro. A
special hour- long TV show was aired and the investigating detectives of
the Green Ribbon Task Force of the Niagara Regional Police (the name taken
after French's schoolmates' symbol of hope) appeared, alongside forensic
scientists. 

Evidence was presented, along with an artists' drawing of one of the
suspects, a re-enactment of the abduction (in the style of an America's
Most Wanted segment) and a presentation of a United States Federal Bureau
of Investigation suspect profile that suggest the perpetrators were two
males, one of which "worked with power tools, or in a metalworking shop."
This profile later proved grossly inaccurate and much time was wasted in
its pursuit.  A bogus call to a Burlington 911 operator, alleging that the
caller and an accomplice had murdered French, was made about this time.
This later proved false.

d) The Arrest

The big break came when it was announced that Paul Kenneth Bernardo had
been arrested in Toronto on 43 charges of rape and sexual assault, in
connection with the Scarborough Rapist case. There were rumors that he
might be charged with the French-Mahaffy murders but nothing seemed to come
of it. This 'big break' was actually a hinderance to the Green Ribbon Task
Force. We must go back in time and look at the series of events that
culminated in the arrest to fully understand what happened.

Metropolitan Toronto Police had interviewed Bernardo in November, 1990
regarding the Scarborough Rapist case and asked Bernardo for a saliva
sample, which was to be sent to a Finland lab for testing.   Metro Police
flagged Bernardo's name on the national police computer system, CPIC.  If
Bernardo's name was ever used in a search of the system, Metro would be
notified.

In January 1993,  Niagara Police were investigating an alleged assault on
his then wife Karla Homolka, a veterinarian assistant at the Martindale
Animal Clinic in St. Catharines.  They ran Bernardo's name on CPIC.  The
flag was triggered and Metro Toronto Police were notified.  It was about
this time that Paul Kenneth Bernardo had his name legally changed to Paul
Jason Teale.

The reason for the name change is unclear.  Some think it might have been
an attempt to distance himself from the crimes, while the most likely
explanation is that Bernardo wanted to distance himself from his father,
Kenneth Walter Bernardo, who still lived in Paul's boyhood home on Sir
Raymond Drive, West Hill (Scarborough).  The elder Bernardo was convicted
of sexual assault on September 1, 1992, and jailed for nine months in
February 1993.  The charged involved assaults that took place on two women
between January 1, 1969 and June 30, 1974.  The assaults did not include
sexual intercourse.

Back to our story.  A month later, Metro interviewed Van Smirnis, who was
best man at Bernardo's wedding, regarding Bernardo and the Scarborough
Rapist case.  Smirnis had come forward in the past to suggest that Bernardo
was involved in the rapes.

Meanwhile, in St. Catharines,  Homolka, the alleged victim of wife assault,
confessed to her aunt and uncle, Patti and Calvin Seger, that she had
played a role in the Mahaffy and French murders.   A lawyer was hired and
she commenced initial stages of cooperating with Metro Police regarding, at
least, the two schoolgirl killings.  Later on, Karla's plea bargain
negotiations were conducted between Inspector Vince Bevan of the Green
Ribbon Task Force, and the representative of the Attorney General of
Ontario,  Crown Prosecutor Murray Segal.  The Task Force, in cooperation
with the FBI, began 24 hour surveillance of Bernardo on both sides of the
border.

Metro Police, at the same time,  were convinced that Bernardo was the
Scarborough Rapist (whether from a leak in Green Ribbon or from the Smirnis
interviews), and demanded the arrest of Bernardo, regardless of any ongoing
investigation.  If Niagara would not cooperate, they said they would make
the arrest themselves.

When another leak occurred, this time to the media, that Metro had found
the Scarborough rapist, the race was on.  News organizations beat a hasty
path to 57 Bayview Drive, Port Dalhousie (a northern, lakefront suburb of
St. Catharines), keeping pace with police who were about to close in.
Finally, on February 17, 1993, Paul Kenneth Bernardo (AKA Paul Jason Teale)
was arrested and charged in the Scarborough Rapist case.

The next day, it was announced that it was just a matter of time before he
was charged with the murders. Time went on while detectives sealed off
Bernardo's house and searched it for over a month. A leased, gold colored
Nissan 240SX, Ontario license plate number 660 HFH, was impounded.  This
type of car closely matched the elusive 'cream colored Camaro' sought in
the French investigation. Bernardo/Teale was finally charged with the two
schoolgirl murders on May 10, 1993.

Police announced that they still had a second suspect under surveillance
for the murders but that this person did not pose a threat to the public.
This suspect turned out to be Bernardo's wife, Karla Homolka.   She
provided police with a confession and pointed them in the direction of some
evidence.  She is also expected to testify at the murder and manslaughter
trial of Paul Bernardo, currently scheduled for May 1995.

e) Homolka Is Charged

On May 18, 1993, Karla Homolka Teale was charged with two counts of
manslaughter in the deaths of French and Mahaffy. Judge Francis Kovacs was
selected for the Homolka manslaughter trial.  Karla Homolka entered into
plea bargain discussions with Murray Segal. The contents of this deal will
be dealt with in a later section entitled "The Deal With The Devil".

Pre-trial motions began on June 28, 1993. Crown Counsel Murray Segal argued
that a publication ban should be put into place in order to ensure a fair
trial for Paul Bernardo.  Bernardo's lawyer at the time, Timothy Breen, who
had originally asked for the ban, opposed Segal, charging that police have
deliberately leaked evidence to make his client look guilty and to portray
Homolka as a victim.  Homolka's lawyer, George Walker, spoke in favor of
the ban because of 'psychological damage' that publication would cause the
community.  Next were lawyers for the media claiming that the public's
right to know what goes on in court is being jeopardized.

f) The Publication Ban

After a recess over the Canada Day weekend, on July 5, Kovacs rendered his
decision.  Everyone present at her trial - including representatives of the
news media - was banned from publishing any evidence or details of the
murders. The public was excluded from the courtroom, as were American and
other foreign journalists.  The only people allowed access, other than the
court staff, the accused, counsel and the families of the accused and the
victims were 'accredited Canadian media'.  Author Stephen Williams, who is
writing a book on the case, was barred until the last minute when he
received accreditation from an unknown news outlet.

According to published reports, Crown Murray Segal read a 27 minute Agreed
Statement of Fact into the Court record.  Graphic, shocking facts. (This
