6) What has been happening in the mainstream media with regards to the case
(Radio/Television/Newspapers/Magazines/Books)?

a) The London Sunday Mirror

On September 19, 1993, the London Sunday Mirror ran a story about the
Homolka trial that apparently violated the publication ban.  Entitled
"Killer Ken and Barbie's Video Of Horror",  it was quickly recalled in
Canada by it's distributer (after threats of prosecution) and shredded.

b) Frank Magazine

Frank Magazine, a satirical rag out of Ottawa, ran several 'ban breaking'
stories in the Fall of 1993.  People said that the rumors were basically
reprints of past alt.fan.karla-homolka FAQs.

c) A Current Affair

The US tabloid show "A Current Affair" has covered the case in three
episodes, on October 26, 1993,  November 9, 1993, and July 26, 1994.  All
three times most Canadians were not able to watch these shows due to either
substitution of programming by border stations, or by cable blackouts cause
by cable operators under threat from the Attorney General of Ontario.  The
way in which cable companies blacked out US TV programs was reminiscent of
the way some former Eastern Bloc countries were jamming foreign
programming.  Under international agreement by the International
Telecommunications Union, as well as under the Broadcasting Act and the
Cable Television Regulations, 1986, this is illegal, and so is the
government or any other agency regulating signals that originate outside
their boundaries.  

However, when one cable customer complained to the Canadian
Radio-television and Telecommunications Commission, they were told that the
CRTC was "of the opinion that it should not second guess the actions taken
by a licensee to avoid contempt of court, as long as those actions are
reasonable...in light of the above, the Commission does not intend to take
further regulatory action at this time."

d) The Washington Post

The Washington Post ran a story on November 23, 1993 which broke the ban.
