9) Why are we opposing the publication ban?

There are several reasons to oppose the publication ban.

a) The publication ban is unenforceable. People close to the case - court
witnesses, policemen, medical personnel - have been silently passing on
information to other people, some of which is included in this FAQ. Anybody
with a VCR, photocopier or a modem can just copy the offending information,
and pass it on to a neighbor or friend.
 
If you take the position that the Internet constitutes a publication to
begin with, this file alone is proof of the ineffectiveness of the ban.
Articles that have been removed from Canadian libraries or seized at the
border have been posted here regularly since this newsgroup's creation.
Before newspapers in Buffalo and Detroit had even reprinted the Washington
Post article, it had already been posted to thousands of sites through this
newsgroup. Attempts to remove it from Canadian sites have already failed,
as material has been crossposted into several other newsgroups. And there
are ways to receive this newsgroup at sites where it has been removed.
Internet's electronic superhighway has irrevocably broken the ban, and that
is reason enough to justify lifting it. 

b) This ban has sensationalized this case beyond reasonable proportions,
and has generated huge interest in it not only in Canada, but also the
United States, Britain and other parts of the world. Had the facts in this
case already been disclosed, this story would have disappeared from the
newspaper headlines.  

Look at the example of the trial of Peter Stark, convicted of killing
Whitby, Ontario teen Julie Ann Stanton.  There was one day of particularly
grisly testimony during the trial, but since it was put out into the open,
everyone knew about it, so there were no sensationalism or rumors going
around.      

Opponents may point to the continuing O.J. Simpson case in the United
States, where this former pro football star is on trial for a double
murder.  However, due to the overwhelming press the case has garnered, the
case is proceeding rapidly and very fairly.  If the case was held behind
closed doors, it might have dragged on, or perhaps been subject to
unethical procedures. When a man is facing the death penalty, it is in his,
and the public's, best interest to have everything out in the open.

As it is illegal to publish details about the Homolka trial, fact and rumor
have become inseparable. Much of the information contained in this FAQ and
the Washington Post article come from reliable sources close to the case.
But some of the information related to this case is based solely on rumor
and hence unreliable. People will be asking whether the rumors are still
true long after the true facts of this case are revealed. 

c) This question assumes that people who know anything about this case are
going to be unreliable jurors, and that only people totally ignorant of the
case can insure that Paul Teale gets a fair trial. This is an insulting
assumption, one that many experts will readily repudiate. In addition, Paul
Teale's own lawyer contested this ban, and argued that the prosecuting
attorney had made a secret deal with Homolka, which is indeed the case.

d) Even the judiciary have come out against the ban. Former Supreme Court
judge Willard Estey said in an April 21 speech that bans on high- profile
cases should cease because they don't work.  He said that jurors should be
exposed to various information and not let it affect their legal judgment.
His colleagues that sit on the bench now have emphasized this in the
Dagenais v. CBC decision.

e) Finally, the editor is not concerned with 'grizzly details'.  He has
taken the time to seek some legal opinion and excerpt from criminal law to
illustrate his case.  This FAQ has been written carefully, objectively
enough to state the case against the ban and censorship. 

* * *

10) What legal documents and decisions affect the Internet/USENET with
regard to this ban?

a) Canadian Charter of Rights and Freedoms:

     2) Everyone has the following fundamental freedoms:  a) freedom of
          conscience and religion; b) freedom of thought, belief, opinion
          and expression, including freedom of the press and other media of
          communication; c) freedom of peaceful assembly; and d) freedom of
          association.

The above section of the Charter is probably the strongest argument at the
invalidity of the publication ban.  Reinforced by the Supreme Court of
Canada in Dagenais v. CBC,  fundamental freedoms are of equal standing with
any other rights in the Charter.

b) Criminal Code of Canada:

The Criminal Code definition of publication is very narrow:

     "Newspaper"

297       In section 303, 304, 308, "newspaper" means any paper, magazine
          or periodical containing public news, intelligence or reports of
          events, or any remarks or observations thereon, printed for
          sale and published periodically or in parts or numbers, at
          intervals not exceeding thirty-one days between the publication
          of any two such papers, parts or numbers, and any paper magazine
          or periodical printed in order to be dispersed and made public,
          weekly or more often, or at intervals not exceeding thirty-one
          days, that contains advertisements, exclusively or principally.

(NB: Sections 303, 304, and 308 deal with libel.)

This FAQ is neither for sale, nor contains advertising.  It's not revised
every 31 days or less, either.  It is not a newspaper. 

Now, take a look at what section Judge Kovacs invoked the publication ban under:

     Exclusion of public in certain cases -- Protection of child witnesses
     -- Support person -- Witness not to be a support person -- No
     communication while testifying -- Reasons to be stated -- Testimony
     outside courtroom --  Condition of exclusion -- Accused not to
     cross-examine child witness -- Order restricting publication --
     Mandatory order on application -- Failure to comply with order

486 (1)   Any proceedings against an accused shall be held in open court,
          but where the presiding judge, provincial court judge or justice,
          as the case may be, is of the opinion that it is in the interest
          of public morals, the maintenance of order or the proper
          administration of justice to exclude all or any members of the
          public from the court room for all or part of the proceedings, he
          may so order.

The 'failure to comply' clause of this section deals only with subsection
3, (which deals with sex offenses, extortion and usury).  Kovacs didn't use
3, he used 1.  He issued the publication ban based upon no statutory
authority whatsoever, but upon a court's claimed "inherent jurisdiction"
over press coverage.  Kovacs pointed this out in this excerpt from the ban
ruling:

[para 96] That authority at common law continues in force under s.8(2) of
the Code as Watt J. in the Scientology case pointed out.  While Watt J. was
dealing with an application for a ban of publication by a co-accused, his
remarks as to the common law authority of courts to regulate their
proceedings and ensure justice are of general application.

(S. 8(2) states: "The criminal law of England that was in force in a
province before April 1, 1955 continues in force in the province except as
altered, varied, modified or affected by this Act or any other Act of
Parliament of Canada.")

Not only is the common law authority based on an entirely different case
[Church of Scientology of Toronto v. The Queen (no. 6) (1986) 27 C.C.C.
(3d) 193 (H.C.J.), a case where Toronto police raided the offices of the
Church and carted away files], Kovacs applies this rule to the supposed ban
application by Bernardo (who dropped his request for the ban before the
hearing). Also, no one has as yet been able to give a concise explanation
of s.8(2).

If Kovacs had been the trial judge at a jury trial, he could have used two
parts of the Criminal Code, s.648(1) in combination with s.647(1) to ban
any of the proceedings until the jury had retired.  If there had been a
preliminary hearing for Homolka, he could have used s.539(1) to ban
publication until the accused was discharged, or the trial ended.  However,
since there was neither a jury nor a preliminary hearing, Kovacs had not
statute authority to place a ban on proceedings.

c) Ministry of the Attorney General Procedures

Another flaw in the Attorney General's crusade against ban breaking is that
she is disregarding her own rules in the process:

"Amongst other things, the Attorney General is required to state, both in
court and in public and to reveal on request, the full and complete
particulars of any and all discussions of Crown agents and Attorneys in
respect of any procedure of "Charge Screening, Disclosure, and Resolution
Discussions (Plea Bargaining)" and there are many, many rules and
situations under which the Attorney General is
permitted/required/prohibited from commencing/continuing/withdrawing
charges and another 2,000 conditions upon the various terms and conditions
of a "resolution" many be reached, after it has been determined that the
Commencement, Continuance, and non- Withdrawal of charges is the
appropriate course of action....They are required to produce, without
exception or editing or blacking out, the entire, complete and unabridged
transcripts of all the evidence, statements, and all the files regarding
the analysis of the matter by the Attorney General from the time of the
initial arrest right up to a nd including the trial.  All matters relating
to charge screening procedures, legal advice to the Attorney General, and
the like must be disclosed."

[Thanks to Michael Wass (michael_wass@tvo.org), Keith Medcalf
(kmedcalf@io.org), and Roy Brander (branderr@cuug.ab.ca) for this section.]

d) Dagenais v. Canadian Broadcasting Corporation [1994] (No. 23403)

In a landmark ruling handed down December 8, 1994, the Supreme Court of
Canada has suddenly made it very hard to issue a standard publication ban.
In a 6-3 decision, it ruled that the controversial National Film Board
movie "The Boys Of St. Vincent" should not have been banned from broadcast
on CBC stations in Ontario and Montreal. The court went on to comment on
publication bans in general, and issues some new precedents. Chief Justice
Antonio Lamer wrote the decision for the majority, an excerpt of which
appears below:

"The pre-Charter common-law rule governing publication bans emphasized the
right to a fair trial over the free expression interests of those affected
by the ban. In my view, the balance this rule strikes is inconsistent with
the principles of the Charter, and in particular, the equal status given by
the Charter to ss. 2(b) and 11(d). It would be inappropriate for the courts
to continue to apply a common-law rule that automatically favoured the
rights protected by s. 11(d) over those protected by s. 2(b). A
hierarchical approach to rights, which places some over others, must be
avoided, both when interpreting the Charter and when developing the common
law. When the protected rights of two individuals come into conflict, as
can occur in the case of publication bans, Charter principles require a
balance to be achieved that fully respects the importance of both sets of
rights."

". . .I am, therefore, of the view that it is necessary to reformulate the
common-law rule governing the issuance of publication bans in a manner that
reflects the principles of the Charter. Given that publication bans, by
their very definition curtail the freedom of expression of third parties, I
believe that the common-law rule must be adapted so as to require a
consideration both of the objectives of a publication ban, and the
proportionality of the ban to its effect on protected Charter rights. The
modified rule may be stated as follows:  A publication ban should only be
ordered when: (a) Such a ban is necessary in order to prevent a real and
substantial risk to the fairness of the trial, because reasonably available
alternative measures will not prevent the risk; and (b) The salutary
effects of the publication ban outweigh the deleterious effects to the free
expression of those affected by the ban. It must be noted, however, that
while the Charter provides safeguards both against actual instances of bias
and against situations that give risk to a serious risk of a jury's
impartiality being tainted, does not require that all conceivable steps be
taken to remove even the most speculative risks. "

"...It should also be noted that recent technological advances have brought
with them considerable difficulties for those who seek to enforce bans. The
efficacy of bans has been reduced by the growth of interprovincial and
international television and radio broadcasts available through cable
television, satellite dishes, and shortwave radios. It has also been
reduced by the advent of information exchanges available through computer
networks. In this global electronic age, meaningfully restricting the flow
of information is becoming increasingly difficult. Therefore, the actual
effect of bans on jury impartiality is substantially diminishing."

". . .In order to provide guidance for future cases, I suggest the
following general guidelines for practice with respect to the application
of the common-law rule for publication bans: (a) At the motion for the ban,
the judge should give the media standing (if sought) according to the rules
of criminal procedure and the established common-law principles with regard
to standing. (b) The judge should, wherever possible, review the
publication at issue. (c) The party seeking to justify the limitation of a
right (in the case of publication ban, the party seeking to limit freedom
of expression) bears the burden of justifying the limitation.  The party
claiming under the common-law rule that a publication ban is necessary to
avoid a real and serious risk to the fairness of the trial is seeking to
use the power of the state to achieve this objective. A party who uses the
power of the state against others must bear the burden of proving that the
use of state power is justified in a free and d emocratic society.
Therefore the party seeking the ban bears the burden of proving the
proposed ban is necessary, in that it relates to an important objective
that cannot be achieved by a reasonably available and effective alternative
measure, that the proposed ban is as limited (in scope, time, content,
etc.) as possible, and there is a proportionality between the salutary and
deleterious effects of the ban. At the same time, the fact that the party
seeking the ban may be attempting to safeguard a constitutional right must
be borne in mind when determining whether the proportionality test has been
satisfied. (d) The judge must consider all other options besides the ban
and must find that there is no reasonable and effective alternative
available. (e) The judge must consider all possible ways to limit the ban
and must limit the ban as much as possible; and (f) The judge must weigh
the importance of the objectives of the particular ban and its probable
effects against the importance of the particular expression that will be
limited to ensure that the positive and negative effects of the ban are
proportionate."

e) Cubby v. Compuserve (US) (90 Civ. 6517, SDNY)

While it has not been established in concrete law that USENET and Internet
are common carriers, in the United States, Cubby v. Compuserve gave the
computer network a  'pseudo' common carrier definition by a judge on
October 31, 1991:

"New York (NB) --U.S. District Judge Peter Leisure ruled that Compuserve
cannot be held liable for information in a newsletter it did not originally
publish.  His decision, the first of it's kind, held that computer
databases are the equivalent of newsstands or bookstores, whose owners
cannot be held liable for the content of the papers they sell unless they
know beforehand that the stories are false."

Leisure's decision took in to account the ruling of the obscenity case
Smith v. California, in which a court held that bookstore owners are not
liable for the content of their wares.  Since it is not uncommon for
Canadian judges to cite U.S. law as president in ruling, hopefully Cubby v.
Compuserve will be taken notice of in Canada.

f) Edward L. Greenspan, Q.C.

Back now in Canada,  Edward L. Greenspan, Q.C. (a noted criminal lawyer)
spoke on this issue:

"Toronto (CUP) --  Calling up the newsgroup files on the Internet (an
international computer network) may defeat the purpose of the ban but not
the ban itself, Greenspan said in an interview    this week. "I don't see
anything wrong with individual members of the public defeating the spirit
of the ban", he said. The confusion arises because people think there is a
ban on the details of the trial, but there is only a ban on publishing that
information, said Greenspan."

g) Telephone Guevremont Inc. v. Quebec (Regie des telecommunications),
[1994] 1 S.C.R. 878

In another case, The Supreme Court of Canada, in a ruling, made it clear
that the federal government has complete jurisdiction over communications.
Although the CRTC has failed to act up until now regarding the cable
blackouts, they could tomorrow force the Attorney General to stop harassing
cable companies with hollow threats of prosecution:

"We are all of the view that Telephone Guevremont Inc. is an
interprovincial work and undertaking within the legislative authority of
the Parliament of Canada by virtue of ss. 92(10)(a) and 91(29) of the
Constitution Act, 1867 by reason of the nature of the services provided and
the mode of operation of the undertaking, which provides a
telecommunication signal carrier service whereby its subscribers send and
receive interprovincial and international communications as set out in the
reasons of Rousseau-Houle J.A.  The constitutional question is answered as
follows:

Question 1:         Is Telephone Guevremont Inc. a work and undertaking
                    within the legislative authority of the Parliament of
                    Canada by virtue of ss. 92(10)(a) and 91(29), of the
                    opening words of s. 91 or otherwise of the Constitution
                    Act, 1867?

Answer:             Yes.

(Sections 91 and 92 of the Constitution Act, 1867 refer to the division of
powers between the provinces and the Federal Government.)

